<Project>

  <!--
    Boundary for vendored/third-party source trees (git submodules).
    Deliberately does NOT import the repo-root Directory.Build.props/targets:
    upstream projects keep their own package versions, warning policy and
    TargetFrameworks. Central Package Management and TreatWarningsAsErrors
    must not leak into external code.

    WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT THIS LEVEL
    ---------------------------------
    MSBuild imports the NEAREST Directory.Build.props walking up from the
    project directory, and imports ONLY that one. The root
    Directory.Build.props therefore never reaches a tree that has its own
    copy of this file here. That is what makes the boundary work: the
    vendored tree stops the walk at contrib/external/ and the root policy
    (CPM, TreatWarningsAsErrors, the reflection ban, the architecture gate)
    applies to Harbor's own code only.

    RULE FOR ANY FUTURE VENDORED TREE
    ---------------------------------
    A third-party tree vendored into this repository MUST get the same three
    files, one directory level above the vendored tree's root, so the walk
    stops before it reaches the repository root:
      <vendored-root>/../Directory.Build.props     (this file)
      <vendored-root>/../Directory.Build.targets
      <vendored-root>/../Directory.Packages.props
    Without them the vendored source inherits the root's CPM pin (every
    PackageReference must then carry an explicit version, and NuGet's
    transitive pinning rejects the upstream edges) and the root's
    TreatWarningsAsErrors (upstream warnings become Harbor build failures).
  -->

</Project>
